-- 293: what the sources actually say
--
-- Migration 292 shipped ks3-24 with five sources and no adversarial
-- verification pass. Four verifiers were then run against the primary
-- documents, one per source cluster. They returned 14 confirmations, 14
-- corrections and 4 demotions. This migration carries every correction into
-- the live rows, because 292 is already applied and the wrong version is what
-- a school would teach from today.
--
-- THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST. The lesson's central pivot was reversed. We told
-- teachers, on a slide, that a computer reader is barred from the GCSE English
-- Language reading paper. JCQ says the exact opposite, by name: "A computer
-- reader will be allowed in papers (or sections of papers) testing reading."
-- What is barred there is a HUMAN reader, because a person reading aloud adds
-- vocal interpretation and the interpretation is what the paper marks. The bar
-- also falls on SECTIONS, not whole papers, and a candidate who would normally
-- have a reader gets up to 50 percent extra time in that section instead.
--
-- That was a regulatory claim stated to the one audience that could act on it,
-- and a SENCO could have been told it. The corrected pivot is better and more
-- on thesis than the wrong one: what flips is not the technology, it is
-- whether the help hands over the thing being tested.
--
-- THE THREE HEADLINE NUMBERS SURVIVED. Bastani's 48, 127 and 17 are all
-- correct, correctly signed, and correctly assigned to arm and to phase. Every
-- defect there was in framing: the trial was cluster randomised across about
-- fifty classes at ONE Turkish school with pupils aged roughly 14 to 17, not
-- "Years 10 and 11"; and the hints arm came out statistically indistinguishable
-- from control rather than ahead of it, with the paper explicitly observing no
-- positive effect. "Came out ahead" appeared eight times and is now gone.
--
-- THE OPENING HOOK HAD THE WRONG DENOMINATOR. The 72 percent who declined AI
-- is from Oxford University Press's 704 pupil qualitative strand, not its 3,100
-- pupil survey, and the task was a paragraph about AI itself inside a study,
-- which plausibly inflates it. The slide claimed three thousand pupils, over
-- four times the real base.
--
-- AND THE LIVE DEMO WOULD HAVE FAILED. The illusion of explanatory depth is
-- "far stronger for explanatory knowledge than many other kinds of knowledge,
-- such as that for facts, procedures or narratives", and Mills and Keil found
-- no illusion for procedures in children at all. "Pick something you learned
-- this week" mostly yields facts and dates, which is exactly where the effect
-- is weak, so the drill is now constrained to something that works in steps.
--
-- SEPARATELY, AND FOUND BY RENDERING THE PAGE RATHER THAN READING IT: four
-- teacher_notes fields shipped in the wrong SHAPE. subject_knowledge was a
-- plain string where the lesson page maps over {heading, body}; evidence_base
-- and parent_questions were lists of strings; hard_questions was keyed {a,q}
-- instead of {question,answer}. The page guards with `?.length`, and a non
-- empty string has a length, so it passed the guard and threw on .map. The
-- teacher prep page for this module has been returning 500 in production since
-- 292 applied. scripts/check-module-contract.mjs now checks the shape of all
-- four, mutation tested seven ways.
--
-- ks3-22 carried the same Bastani overstatement in its teacher notes and is
-- corrected in the same pass.
--
-- ONE THING WORTH KEEPING FOR THE NEXT MIGRATION THIS SIZE. This row went in
-- chunked, so it was digest checked rather than trusted: every string leaf,
-- sorted, joined and md5ed on both sides. The first comparison FAILED, and the
-- content was fine. Postgres sorts under the database collation and Python
-- sorts by codepoint, so `order by s` and `sorted()` disagree on the same set
-- of strings. Use `order by s collate "C"` and the two agree. Migration 292
-- verified itself with the unsafe default and happened to pass, which is worse
-- than failing: it means that check was never actually testing what it claimed.

update schools.school_lessons set
  slides = '[{"type": "title", "phase": "starter", "minutes": 1, "character": "orbit", "eyebrow": "KS3 . Years 7 and 8", "title": "Is it doing my thinking?", "body": "Not whether you use it. Where you let it in.", "script": "Read the title, then the line under it, then stop. Do not open with a warning and do not open with a survey of who uses it. The whole lesson turns on the second line: nobody in this room is being asked to give anything up."}, {"type": "objective", "phase": "starter", "minutes": 2, "outcome": "I can decide, for one piece of work, which part I have to do myself.", "why": "You are going to be using these tools for the rest of your life. Nobody has taught you where to put them.", "gains": ["A test you can run on any task in about ten seconds", "A way to find out whether you actually learned it", "The reason your brain feels certain when it is wrong"], "script": "Land the outcome as a decision, not a rule. They are not being told what to do with AI. They are being handed the thing that lets them decide, task by task, which is a bigger offer and they will notice."}, {"type": "discussion", "phase": "starter", "minutes": 3, "mode": "pairs", "seconds": 90, "prompt": "Hundreds of pupils a bit older than you were asked to write a paragraph about AI, and they were told they could use an AI tool to help if they wanted. Guess: how many out of every ten chose not to use it?", "lookFor": "Guesses are almost always low, usually two or three. The real answer is about seven.", "script": "Take guesses before you say the number. Then tell them: 72 percent chose not to use it. Let that sit. Most of them have spent two years being talked to as though they are the one child in ten who hands in work they did not write. Say plainly that this lesson is not about that child. The study is Oxford University Press, June 2026: 704 pupils across 20 schools, and of those offered an AI tool for the task, 72 percent left it alone. Two honest limits if a pupil pushes: the task was a paragraph about AI itself, inside a study, which probably pushes the number up, so treat seven in ten as the ceiling rather than the everyday rate."}, {"type": "keywords", "phase": "starter", "minutes": 2, "heading": "Three words for today", "words": [{"word": "offload", "definition": "To hand a job to something outside your head. A calculator, a note, a friend, a chatbot. Offloading is not cheating. Everybody does it every day."}, {"word": "fluent", "definition": "Smooth and easy. Watch this one. Work that feels smooth while you do it is often the work you remember least."}, {"word": "calibrated", "definition": "When how sure you feel matches how right you actually are. Today you find out whether yours is."}], "script": "Offload is the load bearing word and it must land as neutral. If offloading were bad, writing anything down would be bad. Fluent is the trap being named early so it pays off in cycle three."}, {"type": "concept", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "emoji": "🧪", "heading": "The help that costs you something", "body": "Nearly a thousand secondary pupils, aged about 14 to 17, had their maths classes split into three groups. One group had no AI. One had a normal chatbot. One had a chatbot told to give hints and never the answer. Then the tool was taken away and everyone sat the same exam.", "script": "Set it up as an experiment and hold the result back. Do not say what happened. The next two slides are the payoff and they only work if nobody has been told yet. If a pupil asks where: one large secondary school in Turkey, autumn 2023. Nobody in that study was as young as this class, so we are borrowing a result from older pupils, and saying so out loud is the same habit the lesson is teaching."}, {"type": "choice", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "question": "Before you see the result. On the exam, with the tool taken away from everyone, which group came out WORST?", "options": [{"text": "The group who had the normal chatbot", "correct": true, "feedback": "Yes, and they had done best in every practice session. On the exam they came 17 percent BELOW the group who never had it at all."}, {"text": "The group told to give hints and never answers", "correct": false, "feedback": "They finished level with the group who never had AI. No penalty, and no bonus either. The hints did not make them learn more, they stopped them learning less."}, {"text": "The group with no AI at all", "correct": false, "feedback": "They are the line the other two are measured against. One group fell below them, one group matched them, and nobody beat them."}], "script": "Make them commit before you show them anything. Hands up for each. Guessing first is not a warm up, it is the thing that makes the answer stick, and it is worth telling them that."}, {"type": "diagram", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "heading": "Same tool, opposite result", "caption": "What happened while they had it, and what happened when it went.", "steps": [{"emoji": "📈", "title": "Chatbot giving answers", "text": "Practice scores went up 48 percent. On the exam, with the tool gone, they scored 17 percent BELOW the group who never had it."}, {"emoji": "💡", "title": "Chatbot giving hints only", "text": "Practice scores went up 127 percent. On the exam they came out level with the group who never had it. Not ahead of them. Level."}, {"emoji": "⚖️", "title": "Same model, same school", "text": "One instruction was different. Give a hint, never the answer. That was the only change."}], "verdicts": ["The tool was not the problem", "Where the effort went was the problem"], "script": "Give them ten seconds on the two numbers before you speak. The 127 is the one that surprises. The careful arm did not get a smaller boost for being careful, it got a much bigger one, and then it walked away with no damage. Say the limit out loud too: it did not beat the no AI group, it matched them. Guardrails stopped a loss, they did not buy a gain. Also worth knowing that both practice numbers were scored while they still had the tool in front of them, so they measure performance, not learning. Bastani and colleagues, PNAS, 2025."}, {"type": "concept", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "emoji": "🏋️", "heading": "Why the easy version teaches you less", "body": "Your memory is not a bucket you pour things into. It is more like a muscle: it changes when it has to work. The moment you are stuck and reaching for something is the moment it changes. Skip that moment and the answer still arrives, it just arrives in your book instead of in you.", "script": "Keep the muscle line simple and do not oversell the brain science. The honest version is that effort at the moment of retrieval is what makes memory stick, and that finding is decades old and solid. It does not need a brain scan."}, {"type": "discussion", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "mode": "pairs", "seconds": 60, "prompt": "Think of one thing you are genuinely good at. Who did the hard part while you were learning it?", "lookFor": "They say it themselves. Nobody gets good at something by watching someone else do it.", "script": "Sixty seconds, take three. This is the cycle one landing and they should say the sentence rather than hear it. Football, an instrument, a game, cooking. Nobody names a skill they got by watching."}, {"type": "concept", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "emoji": "🎯", "heading": "What is this task teaching you?", "body": "Here is the thing that decides it, and it is not a rule about AI. Every task is trying to teach you one thing. Find that thing, and let the tool touch everything except it.", "script": "This is the centre of the lesson. Say it slowly, twice. Note the shape: it is a question they answer per task, not an instruction they obey. That is deliberate and it is why it survives outside this room."}, {"type": "diagram", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "heading": "The exam board already decided this", "caption": "Two real rules, same paper, same pupil.", "steps": [{"emoji": "🖥️", "title": "GCSE English, the reading section", "text": "A computer reader IS allowed. A flat machine voice says the words and hands you nothing else."}, {"emoji": "🗣️", "title": "Same paper, same section, a person reading", "text": "A human reader is NOT allowed. A person reading aloud adds emphasis and meaning, and working out the meaning is what that section marks."}, {"emoji": "🔑", "title": "Same pupil, same words out loud, opposite answer", "text": "What flips it is not the person and not the technology. It is whether the help hands over the thing being tested."}], "verdicts": ["A ramp gets you to the work", "A swap does the work"], "script": "This is the one that makes the lesson fair, and it is worth getting exactly right because a teacher may repeat it to a SENCO. A computer reader is permitted, including in a paper testing reading: JCQ allows it by name. What is barred in a section testing reading is a HUMAN reader, because a person reading aloud supplies nuance, and the nuance is the thing being marked. A candidate who would normally have a reader gets up to 50 percent extra time in that section instead. Note it is sections, not whole papers: a human reader is fine in the writing section of the same exam. So say it plainly: a reader is not cheating and never was. Anyone in this room with an access arrangement is using a ramp and the rules agree with them."}, {"type": "choice", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "question": "French vocabulary test on Friday. You ask a chatbot to translate the word list for you. Ramp or swap?", "options": [{"text": "Swap. Turning French into English is the thing being tested", "correct": true, "feedback": "Right. The task is teaching you to hold those words. Handing that over leaves nothing behind."}, {"text": "Ramp. It is only a word list, the real work is memorising it", "correct": false, "feedback": "The memorising starts the moment you work out what the word means. Get the meaning handed to you and you have skipped the first pass."}, {"text": "Neither. It depends how many words there are", "correct": false, "feedback": "The number does not change what is being taught. A hundred words or five, it is the same skill."}], "script": "Quick one. Most rooms get this. It is here to build the habit of asking the question before the harder case lands."}, {"type": "choice", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "question": "Science write up on a practical you did. You cannot get the introduction started, so you ask for a first sentence. Ramp or swap?", "options": [{"text": "Ramp, if the write up is marked on your method and results", "correct": true, "feedback": "Yes, and notice it took reading the mark scheme to answer. A blank page is not the thing being taught. If you were being marked on writing an opening, the answer flips."}, {"text": "Swap. Any words it writes are its words, not yours", "correct": false, "feedback": "That is a fair instinct and it is the safer habit, but it goes further than the rules do. A sentence that gets you moving is a ramp when the writing is not what is being measured."}, {"text": "Ramp. Getting started is always the hardest part", "correct": false, "feedback": "Hard does not mean it is not the lesson. Sometimes the hard part IS the lesson. The question is what is being marked."}], "script": "This one splits a class and it should. Let them argue for a minute. The point is not the right answer, it is that they cannot get there without knowing what the task is for, which is the whole tool doing its job."}, {"type": "discussion", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "mode": "pairs", "seconds": 60, "prompt": "Name a task where the hard part is in the way rather than the point. Then name one where the hard part IS the point.", "lookFor": "Reading a long question in a maths problem. Handwriting neatly in a history essay. Against: working out the maths, choosing the argument.", "script": "Sixty seconds. Take one of each. If a pupil offers their own access arrangement as an example, take it warmly and move on without making it a moment."}, {"type": "concept", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "emoji": "🪞", "heading": "Close the tab and find out", "body": "Here is the part nobody warns you about. When a tool explains something to you, it does not just do the work. It leaves you feeling like you could have done it yourself. The feeling of understanding arrives whether or not the understanding does.", "script": "Slow down here. This is the most useful idea in the lesson and the one they have never been told. It is not a warning about AI, it is a fact about the feeling in their own head, which is far more interesting to a Year 8."}, {"type": "stat", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "figure": "1 in 4", "claim": "Of the UK teenagers who said they were confident they could spot AI generated content, a quarter could not identify the AI image when they were actually tested.", "source": "Ofcom, Children and Parents: Media Use and Attitudes Report 2025/26, 21 May 2026", "script": "Fifty two percent said they were confident. A quarter of those confident ones got the image wrong. It was one image rather than a test with a pass mark, so say got it wrong rather than failed. Ask the room: did those people know they were about to get it wrong? No. That is the whole point. Being wrong feels exactly like being right. One thing to avoid: Ofcom''s base here is teenagers, 13 to 17, so do not say children your age to a Year 7 class. Say people a bit older than you, and the point still lands."}, {"type": "tryit", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "heading": "The explain it back test", "body": "Pick something you learned this week that works in steps. Score out of 5 how well you could explain it. Now say it out loud, every step, as if to a Year 5. Score yourself again.", "script": "Run it live, right now, sixty seconds, out loud in pairs. The works in steps part matters and is worth enforcing: this effect is strong for things with a mechanism and weak or absent for facts, dates and definitions, so steer anyone who picks one of those. Then ask for a show of hands: whose second number was lower? Many hands go up, not all, and a pupil whose score held is not a failed demonstration. Tell them the drop is not them being thick, it is the test working, and it is the only way to find out before a test does it for you."}, {"type": "choice", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "question": "Your second score was lower than your first. What does that actually tell you?", "options": [{"text": "You found the bit you had borrowed instead of learned", "correct": true, "feedback": "Yes. And you found it on a Tuesday for free, instead of in an exam hall. The gap was always there. Now you can see it."}, {"text": "You are worse at this than you thought", "correct": false, "feedback": "You are exactly as good as you were a minute ago. The only thing that changed is that you now know where the edge is."}, {"text": "You explained it badly", "correct": false, "feedback": "Sometimes. But explaining is how you find the hole, so a bad explanation has just done you a favour."}], "script": "Option two is the one to head off hardest. Nobody got worse. They got accurate. Say that phrase and leave it up."}, {"type": "discussion", "phase": "teach", "minutes": 2, "mode": "pairs", "seconds": 60, "prompt": "If the feeling of understanding turns up whether or not you understand, what could you actually trust instead?", "lookFor": "Doing one on your own. Explaining it. Tomorrow, with it shut. Anything that is an attempt rather than a feeling.", "script": "They will get there. The answer is always some version of try one and see. Write the best version on the board in their words and keep it for the recap."}, {"type": "interactive", "phase": "practise", "minutes": 5, "component": "verdict-sort", "caption": "Sort the six together before the sheet.", "config": {"label": "Ramp or swap . tap the class verdict", "verdicts": ["Ramp, it clears the way", "Swap, it does the learning", "Depends what is being marked"], "doneTitle": "All six sorted", "doneEmoji": "⭐", "doneBody": "Same six on the sheet now. This time your verdict needs the reason written next to it.", "posts": [{"handle": "Case 1", "avatar": "📚", "answer": 0, "text": "You are dyslexic. You have a computer read the history source aloud so you can answer questions about what it says.", "why": "Reading is in the way. The task is understanding the source, and that part is still all yours. The exam board permits a computer reader by name."}, {"handle": "Case 2", "avatar": "✏️", "answer": 1, "text": "Maths homework on solving equations. You photograph each question and copy the worked answers.", "why": "Solving is the whole task. Nothing is left for you once the working is handed over."}, {"handle": "Case 3", "avatar": "💬", "answer": 0, "text": "You have written an essay. You ask a chatbot to tell you which paragraph is weakest and why, then you rewrite it.", "why": "It marked, you wrote. The thinking stayed with you and you got feedback you would otherwise wait a week for."}, {"handle": "Case 4", "avatar": "🌐", "answer": 2, "text": "Spanish homework. You write it in English and have it translated.", "why": "If it is a Spanish writing task, that is a swap. If it is a geography project that happens to be in Spanish, it is a ramp. Check what is being marked."}, {"handle": "Case 5", "avatar": "🧠", "answer": 0, "text": "You are stuck on question four and have been for ten minutes. You ask for a hint, not the answer, then carry on.", "why": "This is the arm of the trial that came out unharmed. Stuck for ten minutes is not noble, it is just stuck. A hint puts you back in the work."}, {"handle": "Case 6", "avatar": "📝", "answer": 1, "text": "Book review on a novel you did not read. You ask for a summary and write from that.", "why": "Reading the book was the task. You can produce something that reads well and know nothing, which is exactly the gap this lesson is about."}]}, "script": "Do case one first and do it deliberately. A reader is a ramp and the exam board says so. That settles the room for anyone in it who uses one. Case four is the one to spend time on: the answer genuinely is it depends, and being able to say why is the skill."}, {"type": "tryit", "phase": "practise", "minutes": 5, "heading": "Now on your own work", "body": "Take a real piece of homework you have this week. Write down the one thing it is teaching you. Then write one place AI may help, and one place it must not.", "script": "This is the transfer and it is the part that leaves the room with them. Circulate. If a pupil cannot name what the task is teaching, that is worth knowing and it is often the more useful conversation."}, {"type": "discussion", "phase": "practise", "minutes": 3, "mode": "table", "seconds": 120, "prompt": "Swap sheets. Do you agree with where your partner drew the line? Argue it out.", "lookFor": "Disagreement is the point. Ask them which of them had read the mark scheme.", "script": "Two minutes. The disagreements are more useful than the agreements and you want a couple of them read out."}, {"type": "choice", "phase": "practise", "minutes": 2, "question": "You have been using hints and you can now do the first three steps alone. What changes next time?", "options": [{"text": "Ask for one step less than last time", "correct": true, "feedback": "That is the whole method. Not stopping, not going cold turkey. One step less, once you have earned it twice."}, {"text": "Stop using it now you can do some of it", "correct": false, "feedback": "Too fast. There is real evidence that pulling support away too early leaves people worse off than keeping it. Drop one rung, not the ladder."}, {"text": "Keep going the same way, it is working", "correct": false, "feedback": "It was working. The help that fits you today is too much help next week, and that is a sign you improved."}], "script": "This is the fading rule and it is the answer to do I just stop. Ask for one step less than last time. Put it on the board."}, {"type": "choice", "phase": "prove", "minutes": 2, "question": "One test decides whether a tool is a ramp or a swap. Which is it?", "options": [{"text": "What is this task actually teaching me?", "correct": true, "feedback": "That is it. Everything else in this lesson hangs off that question."}, {"text": "Am I allowed to use it?", "correct": false, "feedback": "Worth knowing, but it is a different question. Plenty of allowed things still do your learning for you."}, {"text": "Would I be embarrassed if my teacher saw?", "correct": false, "feedback": "A decent gut check, and not enough. Embarrassment tracks the rules, not what you are losing."}], "script": "Straight recall of the tool. If this one is shaky the lesson did not land and cycle two is where to go back to."}, {"type": "choice", "phase": "prove", "minutes": 2, "question": "You read an explanation and it made complete sense. How much does that tell you about whether you have learned it?", "options": [{"text": "Almost nothing. Understanding feels the same whether or not it stuck", "correct": true, "feedback": "Exactly. That feeling is the least reliable thing in the room, which is why the explain it back test exists."}, {"text": "Quite a lot. If it made sense, you understood it", "correct": false, "feedback": "It made sense while it was in front of you. The question is what is there tomorrow with the tab shut."}, {"text": "It depends how hard the topic was", "correct": false, "feedback": "The feeling shows up on hard topics and easy ones alike. Difficulty does not make it more trustworthy."}], "script": "This is the one worth getting right. Everything they do afterwards depends on not trusting the feeling of having understood."}, {"type": "recap", "phase": "close", "minutes": 2, "heading": "Three things to take out of here", "points": ["Ask what the task is teaching you, then let it touch everything except that.", "The feeling of understanding turns up whether or not the understanding did. Explain it back and find out.", "Ask for one step less than last time."], "script": "Read them. Do not add to them. Three is what fits in a head on the way to the next lesson."}, {"type": "digi", "phase": "close", "minutes": 2, "heading": "One last thing", "lines": ["Nobody in here is being told to stop using it.", "You are being told where to stand while you do.", "The pupils who kept what they learned used it more than anyone. They just never asked it for the answer."], "script": "Close on the third line and stop. It is the ban neutral position in one sentence and it is the thing they will repeat to someone else. Do not add a warning after it."}]'::jsonb,
  teacher_notes = '{"learning_objective": "To decide, for a given task, which part of the thinking must stay with me and where a tool may legitimately help.", "essential_question": "How do I tell the difference between help that gets me to the work and help that does the work?", "timing": "61 minutes: starter 8, cycle one 10, cycle two 10, cycle three 10, practise 15, prove 4, close 4", "cycles": [{"verb": "Notice", "title": "The help that costs you something", "outcome": "I can say why the group that got answers did worse once the tool was gone.", "minutes": 10}, {"verb": "Test", "title": "What is this task teaching you?", "outcome": "I can sort a ramp from a swap and say what decided it.", "minutes": 10}, {"verb": "Prove", "title": "Close the tab and find out", "outcome": "I can run the explain it back test on myself and read the result honestly.", "minutes": 10}], "tool": {"heading": "The thinking test", "strapline": "Three questions, about ten seconds, any task.", "lines": ["What is this task teaching me?", "Is the hard part the teaching part, or is it in the way?", "If it is in the way, use the tool and say that you used it."]}, "keywords": [{"word": "offload", "definition": "To hand a job to something outside your head, such as a calculator, a note or a chatbot. Offloading is normal and often sensible. The question is only ever which job."}, {"word": "fluent", "definition": "Smooth and easy. Worth watching, because work that felt smooth at the time is often the work that is gone a week later."}, {"word": "calibrated", "definition": "When how sure you feel matches how right you actually are. Most people are badly calibrated after a tool has explained something to them."}], "prior_knowledge": ["Pupils have used a chatbot for schoolwork at least once, which is true of roughly six in ten of this age group.", "Pupils know what a mark scheme is and have seen one for at least one subject."], "key_learning_points": ["The same AI tool improved practice scores and lowered exam scores, and the only difference was whether it gave hints or answers.", "The test that decides it is what the task is teaching you, not whether the tool is allowed.", "Using a tool inflates how well you think you understand, so the feeling of understanding cannot be trusted as evidence.", "Support should be reduced one step at a time once it has been earned, not dropped all at once."], "misconceptions": ["That the lesson is about cheating. It is not. Every case in the practise set is something a pupil is allowed to do, and the question is what it costs them.", "That using AI more is the problem. In the trial, the group that came out unharmed used it as much as the group that came out 17 percent down.", "That a reader, a scribe or text to speech is a soft option. Those are access arrangements, the exam board permits them by name, and this lesson says so explicitly.", "That struggling on your own is always better. If a pupil cannot get started at all, unaided struggle is not learning, it is just failing, and a hint is the correct move."], "i_can": ["I can explain why the group given answers did worse on the exam than pupils who never had the tool.", "I can run the thinking test on a task and say which part must stay with me.", "I can use the explain it back test to find out whether I have actually learned something."], "commitment_stem": "My commitment: the next piece of work I use AI on, the part I will do myself is...", "teacher_tip": "Do not open with a warning. The single most common way this lesson fails is that pupils decide in the first ninety seconds that it is another assembly about cheating and stop listening. The 72 percent figure in the starter exists to prevent exactly that, so do not skip it or soften it.", "equipment": "Projector. Printed pupil booklet. No pupil devices needed. If pupils do have devices, the explain it back test works better with them shut.", "paper_fallback": "Everything runs without screens. The six practise cases are on the sheet, the sort can be done as a three column table on paper or as a stand in three corners activity, and the explain it back test needs nothing at all.", "differentiation": {"support": "Bookmark strip with the three thinking test questions on the table throughout. Do practise cases one and two together using the stem: the task is teaching me... so this is a... Pupils may answer on the page rather than to the room at any point.", "stretch": "Case four is genuinely undecidable without more information. Ask the strongest pupils to write the two versions of the task that would flip the answer, then argue which is more likely and why."}, "send": {"communication": "Every practise case carries a sentence stem on the sheet. The explain it back test may be done to a partner, to an adult, or written, and nobody is required to speak in front of the room.", "attention": "The thinking test is three questions and one decision at a time. Case cards can be handled one at a time rather than as a set of six.", "sensory": "The sort runs on paper if the board version is too busy. No sound is required at any point in this lesson.", "eal": "The thinking test translates cleanly and is worth having in first language on the desk. Note that case four is specifically about working across two languages and is a good entry point for EAL pupils to lead."}, "passport_stage": "shaper", "worksheet": {"title": "Ramp or swap: six cases, then your own homework", "directions": "For each case, write what the task is teaching, then your verdict, then the reason. Finish with one real piece of your own work.", "verdict_options": ["Ramp, it clears the way", "Swap, it does the learning", "Depends what is being marked"]}, "worksheet_items": [{"n": 1, "text": "You are dyslexic. You have a computer read the history source aloud so you can answer questions about what it says.", "stem": "The task is teaching me..."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Maths homework on solving equations. You photograph each question and copy the worked answers.", "stem": "The task is teaching me..."}, {"n": 3, "text": "You have written an essay. You ask a chatbot which paragraph is weakest and why, then you rewrite it yourself.", "stem": "The task is teaching me..."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Spanish homework. You write it in English and have it translated.", "stem": "The task is teaching me..."}, {"n": 5, "text": "You are stuck on question four and have been for ten minutes. You ask for a hint, not the answer, then carry on.", "stem": "The task is teaching me..."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Book review on a novel you did not read. You ask for a summary and write from that.", "stem": "The task is teaching me..."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Your own homework this week. Name it, then draw the line yourself.", "stem": "The task is teaching me..."}], "starter_quiz": {"title": "Before we start", "instructions": "Four questions. Nobody marks these and nobody sees them but you.", "questions": [{"q": "Out of every ten pupils who were offered an AI tool for a writing task, how many chose not to use it?", "options": ["About two", "About four", "About seven"], "answer": 2}, {"q": "If an explanation made complete sense to you, how much does that prove?", "options": ["That you have learned it", "Very little on its own", "That it was an easy topic"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Having a computer read an exam question aloud to a dyslexic pupil is...", "options": ["Against the rules", "Allowed, the exam board permits it by name", "Allowed only in primary school"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "You have been using hints and can now do part of it alone. What should change?", "options": ["Stop using it completely", "Ask for one step less than last time", "Nothing, it is working"], "answer": 1}]}, "exit_quiz": {"title": "Before you go", "instructions": "Four questions. Same four ideas as the start, so you can see what moved.", "questions": [{"q": "In the maths trial, what was the only difference between the group that came out level and the group that came out 17 percent down?", "options": ["How much they used it", "Hints instead of answers", "How good they were at maths already"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "What is the thinking test?", "options": ["Am I allowed to use this?", "What is this task teaching me?", "Would my teacher mind?"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Your second score in the explain it back test was lower than your first. That means...", "options": ["You got worse", "You found the part you had borrowed", "You explained it badly"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "A pupil who uses a computer reader in an exam is...", "options": ["Getting an unfair advantage", "Using a ramp the exam board permits by name", "Better off doing it themselves"], "answer": 1}]}, "evidence_base": [{"claim": "Cluster randomised across about fifty classes, nearly 1,000 pupils in grades 9 to 11 at one Turkish secondary school, autumn 2023. Answers arm plus 48 percent in practice and minus 17 percent on the unaided exam against control. Hints arm plus 127 percent in practice and statistically indistinguishable from control on the exam, with no positive effect observed. Honors classrooms were excluded as non randomly composed, so the analytic sample is below 1,000. Both practice figures were scored while pupils still held the tool, so they measure performance rather than learning.", "source": "Bastani, Bastani, Sungu, Ge, Kabakci and Mariman (2025), Generative AI without guardrails can harm learning: evidence from high school mathematics, PNAS 122(26), e2422633122", "status": "verified"}, {"claim": "72 percent of those offered an AI tool declined it. That figure is from the 704 pupil qualitative strand across 20 schools, not the 3,100 survey, and the task was a short paragraph about AI itself inside a study, so treat it as a ceiling rather than the everyday rate. 15 percent said they had been given enough guidance.", "source": "Oxford University Press (9 June 2026), Navigating AI in Education: Pupils'' perspectives on the role of AI in the classroom", "status": "verified"}, {"claim": "62 percent of pupils said AI had negatively affected their skills. This is a perception item rather than a measured effect, and it belongs to a different report from the 72 percent above.", "source": "Oxford University Press (15 October 2025), Teaching the AI Native Generation, n=2,000 aged 13 to 18", "status": "verified"}, {"claim": "52 percent of teenagers said they were confident recognising AI generated content, and a quarter of those confident teenagers could not correctly identify the AI generated image they were shown. Ofcom''s base is teenagers, so most of a Year 7 class sits below it.", "source": "Ofcom (21 May 2026), Children and Parents: Media Use and Attitudes Report 2025/26", "status": "verified"}, {"claim": "A computer reader is permitted, including in papers or sections of papers testing reading. A human reader is not permitted to read questions or text in a paper or section testing reading, including the reading section of GCSE English Language, where up to 50 percent extra time may be granted instead.", "source": "JCQ, Access Arrangements and Reasonable Adjustments: General and Vocational qualifications, 1 September 2025 to 31 August 2026, March 2026 amended version", "status": "verified"}, {"claim": "Self rated understanding on a 1 to 7 scale falls after producing a step by step causal explanation, a medium effect, Cohen''s d about 0.49 as recomputed by Johnson and colleagues (2016). The effect is specific to causal and mechanistic knowledge rather than facts, procedures or narratives, which is why the classroom drill names something that works in steps.", "source": "Rozenblit and Keil (2002), The misunderstood limits of folk science: an illusion of explanatory depth, Cognitive Science 26(5), 521 to 562", "status": "verified"}, {"claim": "The illusion was found for devices in children in grades 2 and 4, roughly ages 7 to 10, but not in kindergarteners and not for procedures. No study covers 11 to 13 year olds, so applying it to this class is an inference between that result and the adult one.", "source": "Mills and Keil (2004), Knowing the limits of one''s understanding, Journal of Experimental Child Psychology 87(1)", "status": "verified"}], "hard_questions": [{"question": "So is using it cheating or not?", "answer": "Almost everything in this lesson is allowed. Your school will have a rule about what you must declare and you should follow it. This lesson is about something else: what it costs you, which is a question the rules do not answer."}, {"question": "What if I use a reader or a laptop? Is that on the bad list?", "answer": "No, and the exam board agrees with you by name. A reader clears something out of the way so you can get to the thinking. That is a ramp. Nothing in this lesson is aimed at you."}, {"question": "What if I genuinely cannot start without it?", "answer": "Then a hint is the right move, and that is not a failure. Struggling with no way in is not learning, it is just being stuck. Get in with the smallest help that works, then do the rest."}, {"question": "My teachers just ban it. Why are you telling me how to use it?", "answer": "Because you will be using these tools for the next fifty years and somebody has to tell you where to stand. A ban is a rule for this room. This is a method for the rest of it."}], "parent_questions": [{"question": "Are you teaching my child to use ChatGPT for their homework?", "answer": "No, and we are not teaching them to avoid it either. We teach one question they run on each task: what is this piece of work actually trying to teach me? Whatever that is stays with them, and the tool can touch everything else. That is a decision they make per task rather than a rule we hand down, which is why it still works when we are not there."}, {"question": "Would it not be simpler to ban it?", "answer": "It would be simpler and it does not hold. Your child will use these tools for the rest of their life and nobody has shown them where to stand. A ban also pushes it out of sight, so the first time it goes wrong we are the last to hear. The trial we teach from found that the pupils who came out undamaged used AI as much as the ones who came out behind. What differed was what they asked it for."}, {"question": "My child has a reader, a scribe or text to speech. Is this lesson calling that cheating?", "answer": "The opposite, and the lesson says so out loud in the first case the class sorts. Access arrangements are permitted by the exam board by name, and a computer reader is allowed even in a paper that tests reading. They are a ramp to the work, not a substitute for it. If your child uses one, this lesson is the reason they can stop feeling awkward about it."}, {"question": "What should I do at home?", "answer": "The note that went home has it. Do not ask whether they understood their homework, because that feeling is unreliable and this lesson explains why. Ask them to shut the tab and explain it to you, every step. The place they go quiet is the part that was borrowed rather than learned, and the kitchen table is a much cheaper place to find it than an exam hall."}], "subject_knowledge": [{"heading": "The mechanism is effort transfer, not screen time", "body": "Memory is strengthened by effortful retrieval, so a tool that removes the effort removes the strengthening even while the visible output improves. That is why performance during practice and learning measured later can move in opposite directions, which is exactly what the Bastani trial found."}, {"heading": "Why they cannot feel the loss happening", "body": "The second mechanism is metacognitive. Access to a tool inflates a person''s estimate of their own knowledge, so pupils cannot detect the loss by introspection and need an actual test. This is why the lesson runs the explain it back drill live rather than describing it."}, {"heading": "The ramp and swap test is the regulators'' own", "body": "The access versus substitution distinction is not our invention. It is the same test the regulators apply to access arrangements, that an adjustment is refused where it would change what the qualification is measuring. That is why it is both defensible and already embedded in the rules."}, {"heading": "What the exam board actually says about readers", "body": "Worth having straight before a pupil or a SENCO asks. A computer reader is permitted, including in papers or sections testing reading. What is barred in a section testing reading is a HUMAN reader, because a person reading aloud supplies nuance and the nuance is what is being marked. A candidate who would normally have a reader gets up to 50 percent extra time in that section instead. The bar is on sections, not whole papers."}, {"heading": "Where we are extrapolating, and you should say so", "body": "The maths trial was one large Turkish secondary school, pupils aged roughly 14 to 17, cluster randomised across about fifty classes. Nobody in it was the age of this class. The illusion of explanatory depth work covers ages 7 to 10 and adults, with no study on 11 to 13 year olds. Both results are borrowed from either side of this age group, which is reasonable and is still an inference. Saying that out loud models the honesty the lesson is asking for."}]}'::jsonb,
  assessment = '{"retrieval_starter": {"title": "Before we start", "instructions": "Four questions. Nobody marks these and nobody sees them but you.", "questions": [{"q": "Out of every ten pupils who were offered an AI tool for a writing task, how many chose not to use it?", "options": ["About two", "About four", "About seven"], "answer": 2}, {"q": "If an explanation made complete sense to you, how much does that prove?", "options": ["That you have learned it", "Very little on its own", "That it was an easy topic"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Having a computer read an exam question aloud to a dyslexic pupil is...", "options": ["Against the rules", "Allowed, the exam board permits it by name", "Allowed only in primary school"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "You have been using hints and can now do part of it alone. What should change?", "options": ["Stop using it completely", "Ask for one step less than last time", "Nothing, it is working"], "answer": 1}]}, "exit_quiz": {"title": "Before you go", "instructions": "Four questions. Same four ideas as the start, so you can see what moved.", "questions": [{"q": "In the maths trial, what was the only difference between the group that came out level and the group that came out 17 percent down?", "options": ["How much they used it", "Hints instead of answers", "How good they were at maths already"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "What is the thinking test?", "options": ["Am I allowed to use this?", "What is this task teaching me?", "Would my teacher mind?"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Your second score in the explain it back test was lower than your first. That means...", "options": ["You got worse", "You found the part you had borrowed", "You explained it badly"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "A pupil who uses a computer reader in an exam is...", "options": ["Getting an unfair advantage", "Using a ramp the exam board permits by name", "Better off doing it themselves"], "answer": 1}]}}'::jsonb,
  parent_note = '{"headline": "What we taught today, and one thing to try at home", "taught": "Today we looked at where AI belongs in a piece of schoolwork. Not whether to use it, but which part of the thinking has to stay with your child. We used a real trial of nearly a thousand pupils a few school years older than yours where the same chatbot improved practice scores and then lowered exam scores, and the only difference was whether it gave hints or answers. Nobody was told to stop using anything.", "try_tonight": "Do not ask whether they understood their homework. Ask them to shut the tab and explain it to you, every step, as if you knew nothing. The place they go quiet is the part that was borrowed rather than learned, and finding it at the kitchen table is a great deal cheaper than finding it in an exam.", "words": "What was tonight''s homework actually teaching you? Where did you let it help, and where did you keep it out?"}'::jsonb,
  evidence_anchor = 'Bastani et al, PNAS 2025: unguarded AI cost 17 percent on the unaided exam, hints only avoided that loss with no gain over no AI at all',
  statutory_hooks = ARRAY['RSHE 2026 (media literacy and critical evaluation of online content)', 'JCQ Access Arrangements 2025/2026 (computer reader and human reader in reading assessments)']::text[]
where module_id = 'ks3-24-is-it-doing-my-thinking';

-- ks3-22 carried the same overstatement in three strings of its teacher notes.
-- Only those three move. The whole row was digest checked against this file
-- first (148 strings, a15ef8c2696205485bb0d1637c6c9119, identical), so a
-- targeted set is equivalent to a replace here and cannot disturb anything
-- else in a module this change is not about. The where clause makes it a no
-- op if the rows have moved since.
update schools.school_lessons set teacher_notes =
  jsonb_set(jsonb_set(jsonb_set(teacher_notes,
    '{subject_knowledge,2,body}', to_jsonb('One strong trial, Bastani and colleagues in PNAS 2025, gave secondary pupils unrestricted chatbot access and found 17 percent lower exam grades once the tool was taken away. A second arm, given hints instead of answers, came out statistically indistinguishable from pupils with no AI at all, so the guardrails avoided the loss rather than producing a gain. It was cluster randomised across about fifty classes at one Turkish secondary school, pupils aged roughly 14 to 17. That is a claim about unguarded use and learning. It is not a claim about loneliness, attachment or mental health, and it must not be stretched into one.'::text)),
    '{evidence_base,2,claim}',    to_jsonb('Unrestricted chatbot access produced 17 percent lower exam grades, and a hints only arm avoided that loss without beating the no AI group'::text)),
    '{evidence_base,2,source}',   to_jsonb('Bastani et al., PNAS 122(26) e2422633122, 2025'::text))
where module_id = 'ks3-22-when-an-ai-acts-like-a-friend'
  and teacher_notes #>> '{subject_knowledge,2,body}' like '%harm disappearing when guardrails were added%'
  and teacher_notes #>> '{evidence_base,2,claim}'    like '%guardrails removed the harm%';

do $$
declare
  s jsonb; tn jsonb; n int; txt text;
begin
  select slides, teacher_notes into s, tn
    from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks3-24-is-it-doing-my-thinking';
  if s is null then raise exception '293: ks3-24 is missing'; end if;

  -- 1. the deck is intact
  if jsonb_array_length(s) <> 27 then
    raise exception '293: expected 27 slides, found %', jsonb_array_length(s); end if;

  -- 2. the reversed rule is gone, and the real one is present
  txt := s::text;
  if txt like '%Nobody may read it aloud%' then
    raise exception '293: the reversed JCQ claim is still on a slide'; end if;
  if txt not like '%computer reader IS allowed%' then
    raise exception '293: the corrected JCQ rule did not land'; end if;
  if txt not like '%human reader is NOT allowed%' then
    raise exception '293: the human reader half of the rule did not land'; end if;

  -- 3. nobody came out ahead of control, anywhere
  if txt like '%came out ahead%' or tn::text like '%came out ahead%' then
    raise exception '293: "came out ahead" survives, and no arm beat the control'; end if;

  -- 4. the starter no longer claims the survey sample
  if txt like '%Three thousand pupils%' then
    raise exception '293: the starter still claims the 3,100 survey as the 72 percent base'; end if;

  -- 5. the trial is described at its real age band
  if txt like '%Years 10 and 11%' then
    raise exception '293: the trial is still labelled Years 10 and 11'; end if;
  if txt not like '%aged about 14 to 17%' then
    raise exception '293: the real age band did not land'; end if;

  -- 6. the explain it back drill is constrained to a mechanism
  if txt not like '%works in steps%' then
    raise exception '293: the drill is not constrained to causal knowledge'; end if;

  -- 7. THE SHAPES. Each of these four is what 500ed the prep page.
  if jsonb_typeof(tn->'subject_knowledge') <> 'array' then
    raise exception '293: subject_knowledge is %, not an array', jsonb_typeof(tn->'subject_knowledge'); end if;
  if jsonb_typeof(tn->'evidence_base') <> 'array' then
    raise exception '293: evidence_base is not an array'; end if;
  for n in 0 .. jsonb_array_length(tn->'subject_knowledge') - 1 loop
    if (tn->'subject_knowledge'->n->>'heading') is null
    or (tn->'subject_knowledge'->n->>'body') is null then
      raise exception '293: subject_knowledge row % is not {heading, body}', n; end if;
  end loop;
  for n in 0 .. jsonb_array_length(tn->'evidence_base') - 1 loop
    if (tn->'evidence_base'->n->>'claim') is null
    or (tn->'evidence_base'->n->>'source') is null
    or (tn->'evidence_base'->n->>'status') is null then
      raise exception '293: evidence_base row % is not {claim, source, status}', n; end if;
  end loop;
  for n in 0 .. jsonb_array_length(tn->'parent_questions') - 1 loop
    if (tn->'parent_questions'->n->>'question') is null
    or (tn->'parent_questions'->n->>'answer') is null then
      raise exception '293: parent_questions row % is not {question, answer}', n; end if;
  end loop;
  for n in 0 .. jsonb_array_length(tn->'hard_questions') - 1 loop
    if (tn->'hard_questions'->n->>'question') is null
    or (tn->'hard_questions'->n->>'answer') is null then
      raise exception '293: hard_questions row % is not {question, answer}', n; end if;
  end loop;

  -- 8. the exit quiz and starter quiz exist TWICE, in teacher_notes and in
  -- assessment. Correcting one copy and not the other is how "came out ahead"
  -- survived the first correction pass.
  select assessment into s from schools.school_lessons
    where module_id = 'ks3-24-is-it-doing-my-thinking';
  if s->'exit_quiz' <> tn->'exit_quiz' then
    raise exception '293: the two exit quiz copies disagree'; end if;
  if s->'retrieval_starter' <> tn->'starter_quiz' then
    raise exception '293: the two starter quiz copies disagree'; end if;
  if s::text like '%came out ahead%' then
    raise exception '293: the assessment copy still says came out ahead'; end if;

  select slides into s from schools.school_lessons
    where module_id = 'ks3-24-is-it-doing-my-thinking';

  -- 9. the timing string still states what the deck runs
  select sum((v->>'minutes')::int) into n from jsonb_array_elements(s) v;
  if n <> 61 then raise exception '293: deck runs % minutes, expected 61', n; end if;
  if (tn->>'timing') not like '61 minutes%' then
    raise exception '293: timing string disagrees with the deck'; end if;

  -- 10. ks3-22 no longer says the guardrails removed the harm
  select teacher_notes into tn
    from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks3-22-when-an-ai-acts-like-a-friend';
  if tn::text like '%harm disappearing when guardrails%'
  or tn::text like '%guardrails removed the harm%' then
    raise exception '293: ks3-22 still overstates the guardrail result'; end if;

  raise notice '293 ok: 27 slides, 61 minutes, JCQ rule corrected, four shapes fixed, ks3-22 tightened';
end $$;
